/\n4eKGEf/23iRHUV25alT/VikPvXIzds9AgSabJ0kAiikSaHC7eRnd6sDJj9KD\njX60W4C1QfFFfwMU12+Qgik1lLvEMEUUwM5+0BHA6sFnMh9xJhZKaSmO/Vay\ndRAQuHnQ4gnTNRhRPiKeff56RbWulxCmyDliymyDJYZ3Bd95sEA6g7N62NHq\nQJ/caD1knTIE+l7gXnA8XaWaRyxoRrIOi17oU7pF/ykwzgZfOE5Dt/GJMFTz\nx15m4h+opl6gvyZ4N9PsKhC5w2dwgZF8+40ptcwsEwZZf3K6xiSxmjZC7d42\nvVapDnQVnFpIOqZPO9kLI8iSMuLr3tCozrs7MZ3iJ6i50VT4bohAAG7rnJb5\n4diRVk1KfbsQw2oDPSfYs7czr/Kyxkz+ijTXB1CRmajq3fj+pTiaEpR/f5nc\nvafy5PBgWi7yl5cdfNeG47YDUdSm3cNDhRSSH7qnCZbqkXAv2vggACgdodQr\n5B0eQ+xXBrHYeQcrUV5D7/y71JyBjp+f2ZHofgLFHZcKXcuQ8I7+183hgltN\nSYsQebCS1+BfEAChQKaeisqIvZTiuXAwPDHwLoahf47LHU44NcV6s96iWF4v\n33cGLTeH5d95juq/SF8=\n",
    "validation_file": {
      "id": "2",
      "name": "test.py",
      "contents": "import re\nfrom unittest import TestCase\nfrom unittest_runner import ValidationProtocol\n\n\ndef read_code():\n    with open('main.py') as main_file:\n        return main_file.read()\n\n\nclass TestPathGuard(TestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        \"\"\"Run the student's code once and save the results\"\"\"\n        cls.log = ValidationProtocol().get_neighborhood_log()\n        cls.painter = cls.log.painter_logs[0] if cls.log.painter_logs else None\n\n    def need_painter(self):\n        if self.painter is None:\n            self.skipTest(\"Your code didn't run far enough to check yet. Make sure the starter code is still there and run again.\")\n\n    def test_keeps_the_setup(self):\n        \"\"\"Your program keeps the setup that restocks the Painter\"\"\"\n        if len(re.findall(r'take_paint\\s*\\(\\s*\\)', read_code())) < 4:\n            self.fail(\"Keep the four take_paint() lines in the setup. They give the Painter paint for the path.\")\n\n    def test_checks_can_move(self):\n        \"\"\"Your program checks can_move() with an if\"\"\"\n        if not re.search(r'^\\s*if\\s+.*can_move\\s*\\(', read_code(), re.MULTILINE):\n            self.fail(\"Add an if that checks can_move() before the Painter moves, like if can_move():\")\n\n    def test_uses_else(self):\n        \"\"\"Your program uses an else branch\"\"\"\n        if not re.search(r'^\\s*else\\s*:', read_code(), re.MULTILINE):\n            self.fail(\"This level asks you to add an else branch. Add else: after your if, with an indented line that turns the Painter.\")\n\n    def test_painted_the_path(self):\n        \"\"\"Your Painter paints all four squares of the path\"\"\"\n        self.need_painter()\n        self.assertEqual(\n            self.painter.ending_paint_count,\n            0,\n            \"Your Painter should paint all four squares in front of the truck. Keep all four paint(\\\"white\\\") lines.\"\n        )\n\n    def test_reaches_the_end(self):\n        \"\"\"Your Painter ends on the last square of the path\"\"\"\n        self.need_painter()\n        end = self.painter.ending_position\n        self.assertEqual(\n            (end.x, end.y),\n            (5, 1),\n            \"Your Painter should end on the last square of the path, in front of the right end of the truck. Keep all four move() lines, and make sure the guard doesn't skip them.\"\n        )\n\n    def test_faces_the_truck(self):\n        \"\"\"Your Painter turns to face the food truck\"\"\"\n        self.need_painter()\n        self.assertEqual(\n            self.painter.ending_position.direction.lower(),\n            \"south\",\n            \"Your Painter should end facing the food truck, which is south. The else should turn the Painter right. turn_left() only turns left, so think about how the setup lines turned right.\"\n        )",
      "folderId": "0",
      "active": false,
      "type": "validation"
    },
    "teacher_markdown": "Misconceptions and what to test for:\r\n\r\n- The starter crashes on purpose, after painting all four squares. The last move() runs into the planter and the console shows \"Painter tried to move off the grid or into an obstacle.\" Ask students to say what the Painter did wrong before they change anything.\r\n- After the if guard, the error goes away, but the Painter ends facing east, toward the planter. Ask: \"Which way is the Painter facing, and is that the way the truck is?\"\r\n- The else only runs once, at the last square. At the first three squares, can_move() is True and the Painter moves. Ask: \"Which squares ran the if, and which ran the else?\"\r\n- The truck is south of the path. turn_left() only turns left, so a right turn is three left turns, the same idiom the setup lines use. One turn_left() faces north, and two face west. Ask: \"Which way does the Painter face after your else?\"\r\n- Painting happens before the guard, so the last square is painted before the Painter tries to move.\r\n- The block of paint, if, and else is written out four times. If a student asks for a shorter way, say yes, and that the lesson on loops answers it.\r\n- Forgetting the colon after else, or not indenting the line under it, is the most common error.\r\n- Else never takes a condition. Writing else not can_move(): is a syntax error.\r\n- A student who puts move() in the else will crash again. The else runs exactly when the Painter can't move.\r\n- A second if not can_move(): works, but the level asks for an else. Ask: \"What are the two ways to write this?\"\r\n- If a student uses Build Python, they still own the program. Ask them to explain which branch ran at each square.\r\n\r\nValidation checks that the program keeps the four take_paint() lines, uses an if with can_move(), uses an else, paints all four squares (the Painter ends with no paint), ends at the last square of the path, and ends facing south."
  },
